36
00:04:09,200 --> 00:04:11,110
The first one is quite fundamental.

37
00:04:11,110 --> 00:04:16,550
It's about thinking about a productled approach as opposed to a projectled approach.

38
00:04:16,550 --> 00:04:26,469
And today this sounds entirely obvious and I sometimes do stand in front of audiences who look at me and go and you say well you got to remember these things were being thought out in 2011 1213.

39
00:04:26,469 --> 00:04:35,430
I ask the audience for those who are old enough how many of you in 2012 were thinking about building products inside enterprises as opposed to running projects and very few hands go up.

40
00:04:35,430 --> 00:04:48,950
It's more a reminder that actually if you take a product oriented approach and you think about the long-term planning and a product inside an organization that has a particular collection of stakeholders and will have a long-term life and so needs to be managed in a sustainable way.

41
00:04:48,950 --> 00:04:52,390
It drives you to thinking about all your work as building products.

42
00:04:52,390 --> 00:04:57,590
Even if they're never sold outside the organization, even if there's only one copy, it's still a product.

43
00:04:57,590 --> 00:04:59,189
The second principle is really fundamental to architecture in general.

44
00:04:59,199 --> 00:05:04,070
It's about focusing on quality attributes. Most software architects do obsess about quality attributes.

45
00:05:04,070 --> 00:05:08,710
But especially when you're introducing these ideas to people. It's actually not obvious to people.

46
00:05:08,710 --> 00:05:10,310
They think architecture is about boxes and lines.

47
00:05:10,320 --> 00:05:11,909
It's about structure.

48
00:05:11,909 --> 00:05:15,909
The only reason for the structure is to achieve quality attributes.

49
00:05:15,909 --> 00:05:18,390
Security, performance, scalability, resilience, and maintainability.

50
00:05:18,400 --> 00:05:29,430
This is actually an interesting point because often in many software products or projects there is a lot of focus on features and then the quality attributes are often an afterthought.

51
00:05:29,440 --> 00:05:32,469
Exactly. And that's why it's such an important principle.

52
00:05:32,469 --> 00:05:35,909
It's that the quality attributes have to be lived and breathed by the team.

53
00:05:35,909 --> 00:05:40,310
You don't want someone over here called an architect who it's a problem.

54
00:05:40,310 --> 00:05:47,350
And generally speaking, I'd say with all due respect to high performing scrum teams, they can be doing a great job working with their product owner delivering features.

55
00:05:47,350 --> 00:05:55,189
They find it very hard to step back and take that more holistic view unless you remind them to because after all, security is not something that one team can actually achieve.

56
00:05:55,189 --> 00:05:58,390
They need an environment that they plug into to achieve security.

57
00:05:58,390 --> 00:06:04,550
And so that's why it's an architectural principle to focus on quality attributes because it's a holistic systemic characteristic.

58
00:06:04,550 --> 00:06:14,230
The third principle I suspect because again I didn't write it but we can ask Mur I suspect came direct from really the agile manifesto type thinking which is delay decisions as long as you can.

59
00:06:14,230 --> 00:06:17,430
This is a reaction to the kind of architecture that I used to do.

60
00:06:17,440 --> 00:06:18,950
Absolutely.

61
00:06:18,950 --> 00:06:29,270
If you look at the software systems architecture book, I put the graph from that showing architecture activity over time up on slides now so I can laugh at it because it's my graph where we used to say well of course architecture lots

62
00:06:29,270 --> 00:06:37,830
and lots of decisions up front to de-risk the whole enterprise and then we have some decisions at the end where we realize that now we're getting towards production we need to rethink a few things.

63
00:06:37,830 --> 00:06:39,909
Of course, that's a pretty flawed approach.

64
00:06:39,909 --> 00:06:44,629
Actually, as we discovered over the years, some decisions actually do need to be made very early.

65
00:06:44,629 --> 00:06:48,070
And there are contexts where actually more decisions need to be made early.

66
00:06:48,070 --> 00:06:53,189
But the earlier you make a decision, the less information you have, therefore the poorer decision you probably make.

67
00:06:53,189 --> 00:06:55,029
We need to make the decision as late as we can.

68
00:06:55,029 --> 00:06:57,749
As the agile communities have been telling us for is it 25 years now, Jiovali?

69
00:06:57,759 --> 00:07:03,270
Yeah. To make the decision as late as possible because that gives you the most accurate context to make the decision.

70
00:07:03,270 --> 00:07:12,950
This is actually a good point because well I worked in many projects and funnily enough often when people go with early decisions

71
00:07:12,950 --> 00:07:25,430
with grand designs of how the system should be and trying to overthink the evolution and everything what happens is that the systems have this horrible habit of evolving in a direction that is different from the one you forecasted.

72
00:07:25,430 --> 00:07:36,629
So you end up to have to maintain a lot of stuff that you don't need because otherwise you cannot even compile the code plus hack into the system the things that will support the new direction it is going to towards.

73
00:07:36,629 --> 00:07:41,749
So you may end up with a spaghetti monster that you really didn't think of at the beginning.

74
00:07:41,759 --> 00:07:43,670
Exactly.

75
00:07:43,670 --> 00:07:49,830
The number of people over the last 15 years who've told me I just want a futureproof architecture.

76
00:07:49,840 --> 00:07:54,470
pause for a moment before breaking the bad news to them. There's no such thing.

77
00:07:54,479 --> 00:08:01,830
There's just the simplest possible effective architecture for now and we'll maximize the options for change later by keeping it simple.

78
00:08:01,840 --> 00:08:13,670
Now, before letting you proceed, I can tell you this that this is a real thing that happened to me in a requirement document, a requirement that was written that the architecture has to be flexible to support future requirements.

79
00:08:13,670 --> 00:08:21,749
And I explained the person at the time that I didn't have a crystal ball to actually be able to do that in advance.

80
00:08:21,749 --> 00:08:24,390
Yeah, I'll give to a few examples about this.

81
00:08:24,400 --> 00:08:32,630
For example, I mean one experience I had was people were designing the system and they were expecting thousands and thousands of users.

82
00:08:32,630 --> 00:08:39,029
So they built a lot of complex capabilities in the system but it went live and it never reached that user capacity required.

83
00:08:39,029 --> 00:08:42,070
So all that effort of building that was wasted.

84
00:08:42,070 --> 00:08:50,070
And back to your flexibility comment, Giovania, another thing at least maybe used happen quite a lot when rules engines were very popular.

85
00:08:50,070 --> 00:08:53,350
You would say, "Oh, I need the system to be flexible.

86
00:08:53,350 --> 00:09:03,269
That means I'll just write rules and I'll put a rules engine on my system and I'll just change the rules." Which usually didn't work as anticipated and you just put a rules engine while not required.

87
00:09:03,269 --> 00:09:05,590
Well, you could just get by with just some simple logic.

88
00:09:05,590 --> 00:09:12,630
So it's those type of things that if you're thinking you have the most future proof architecture as Owen said and you try to build it,

89
00:09:12,630 --> 00:09:23,670
you'll just waste a lot of time and effort and probably all architectural decisions about you balance different quality attributes and because you build a complex system you might overlook some other quality attributes that you might not have thought of.

90
00:09:23,680 --> 00:09:26,389
Yeah.

91
00:09:26,389 --> 00:09:35,030
And one final comment on this one presented this starchex a lot of people understand it as do not make design decisions and that is not the intent at all

92
00:09:35,030 --> 00:09:45,269
right we want to make design decisions as I said before it is the unit of work of an architect if you do nothing else you just make sure you know which design decisions you're making so it's about making them in the

93
00:09:45,269 --> 00:09:55,990
right context as Owen said yeah so to recap the first three build products or projects quality attributes over functional requirements delay decisions as long as you can.

94
00:09:55,990 --> 00:10:00,470
And then the fourth one is focus on build, test, deploy, and operate.

95
00:10:00,470 --> 00:10:07,590
I think this again is not about only focusing on build, test, deploy, and operate, but it's making sure that someone is thinking about build, test, deploy, and operate.

96
00:10:07,600 --> 00:10:10,949
Again, guessing a little bit reading between the lines.

97
00:10:10,949 --> 00:10:20,870
Historically, software architects perhaps been quite good at debating design patterns and software structures and code line organization and how the unit test should run

98
00:10:20,870 --> 00:10:26,150
and actually have then left a yawning chasm between that and how code runs in operation.

99
00:10:26,150 --> 00:10:35,430
I have a former colleague Edward Butler who I worked with quite a while in Dava who has a a great turn of phrase and Ed started really on the op side of the house and then moved to the dev side of the house.

100
00:10:35,440 --> 00:10:39,110
So he for a period was very influential in DevOps as you might imagine.

101
00:10:39,110 --> 00:10:42,230
and he was one of the pioneers and he had a lovely phrase.

102
00:10:42,230 --> 00:10:51,269
He said on the one side you've got operations on the other side you've got development and in between you have the desert of responsibility where nobody wants to tread because they think they're going

103
00:10:51,269 --> 00:11:01,910
to die and architecting for build test deploy operate is about building the bridge over the desert so that actually code can stop just being beautiful structures and can actually start delivering value in production.

104
00:11:01,920 --> 00:11:07,509
Yeah to me actually this principle is something that is quite interesting because I think is an excellent one.

105
00:11:07,509 --> 00:11:21,430
It also is something that really when you talk about architectures often with architects they don't really emphasize the architects for build test and deploy often there is the emphasis on the boxes and lines if you like yeah I'm simplifying a bit

106
00:11:21,430 --> 00:11:29,829
but it's often on this on the structure usually on the static behavior sometimes some sequence diagrams but then how are we going to test this

107
00:11:29,829 --> 00:11:39,030
or how are we going to build the pretty stuff is never discussed and I think historically there were reasons for that not maybe brilliant reasons but there were practical reasons.

108
00:11:39,030 --> 00:11:44,710
I grew up in a product development and there I mean the development team software engineers didn't deploy anything.

109
00:11:44,710 --> 00:11:51,590
We had to produce a binary that came out of a build cycle that went to show you my age.

110
00:11:51,590 --> 00:11:57,030
Something known as the gold master meaning cuz the first CDs were always gold so you could tell them apart from the other CDs.

111
00:11:57,030 --> 00:11:58,949
So it was stamped the gold master that went to the test team.

112
00:11:58,959 --> 00:12:03,430
And actually that was sort of sane and sensible. This wasn't a cloud deployment.

113
00:12:03,430 --> 00:12:04,949
This was going to have to go to a customer.

114
00:12:04,949 --> 00:12:10,069
the customer was literally going to put that CD into a server machine and try and install the product.

115
00:12:10,069 --> 00:12:16,550
So the fact that you had a separate team with the skills the customer had checking what was coming out of the engineering build cycle actually made a lot of sense.

116
00:12:16,550 --> 00:12:21,670
But as soon as you start operating your own software as a SAS vendor that suddenly doesn't make any sense anymore.

117
00:12:21,670 --> 00:12:24,310
And I think a lot of big companies enterprises were set up that way too.

118
00:12:24,310 --> 00:12:30,150
Do you remember they used to have no well many still do quiet isolated ops function.

119
00:12:30,150 --> 00:12:33,910
Dev are not allowed to have any access or control over production at all.

120
00:12:33,910 --> 00:12:37,829
They can fiddle in their test environments, but there's not a very serious attempt made to sync them up.

121
00:12:37,839 --> 00:12:46,470
And again, something that looks a bit like a gold master comes out of development and is handed to ops across the desert of responsibility.

122
00:12:46,470 --> 00:12:49,829
Ops try and install it, tell you it doesn't work, and hand it back again.

123
00:12:49,829 --> 00:13:02,629
Or even worse, boot it and then hope for the best in full knowledge that it'll probably go wrong and then have to deal with the fallout at half past 2 in the morning when some other region starts using it and they suddenly discover they haven't got their languages loaded.

124
00:13:02,629 --> 00:13:04,470
all whatever gross error has been made.

125
00:13:04,480 --> 00:13:10,629
Also the test bit is actually quite interesting because in practice you can have something

126
00:13:10,629 --> 00:13:22,230
that that high level architecture two systems look identical from a high level point of view same module same things but one is testable the other is not simply because you didn't think about how are you going to test this thing.

127
00:13:22,240 --> 00:13:24,150
Yeah.

128
00:13:24,150 --> 00:13:28,710
So to me also this is quite an important point another one that is often not discussed even nowadays.

129
00:13:28,710 --> 00:13:30,470
No, no, I totally agree.

130
00:13:30,470 --> 00:13:37,509
But I think it's all about back to what we said about the principles about the team and a team owning the software.

131
00:13:37,509 --> 00:13:43,590
So it's not about having separate teams do different things like you build it, you own it, you operate it, right?

132
00:13:43,590 --> 00:13:52,310
In an ideal world, you want the team fully accountable for the software from its conception to its decommission actually at some point in between.

133
00:13:52,310 --> 00:13:54,470
You operate it for many many years hopefully.

134
00:13:54,470 --> 00:14:06,070
And and I think that's sometimes lost on people and this distributed responsibilities of different teams and you create those deserts that O mentioned of no responsibility and testing is another good thing. Right?

135
00:14:06,070 --> 00:14:07,910
Testing should be done by the team.

136
00:14:07,910 --> 00:14:17,110
You might have certain testing experts but you don't need massive separate testing teams and thinking about how you test it, which artifacts you test,

137
00:14:17,110 --> 00:14:26,870
how often you test, what do you mean by regression testing, how you manage your test data, all those things the team should be thinking about. Yeah. And then which principles are left?

138
00:14:26,880 --> 00:14:29,110
There's two.

139
00:14:29,110 --> 00:14:33,750
One is architect for change and the last one is organize your teams along the design of your system.

140
00:14:33,750 --> 00:14:37,189
Um architecting for change is it's a little bit like the testability one.

141
00:14:37,189 --> 00:14:49,910
I think it's if you think about the fact the system will have to undergo possibly traumatic change in the future and you think about that early without building a monstrosity there are things you can do to make it easier to change.

142
00:14:49,910 --> 00:14:52,230
So for example,

143
00:14:52,230 --> 00:15:04,470
I mean your kind of expertise Javanni in domain driven design tends to produce code that's highly modular with very limited dependencies and very regular structure making things testable early frequently allows them to be repackaged in different ways and still testable.

144
00:15:04,470 --> 00:15:19,189
Building from smaller rather than huge big monolithic components anyone I mean I'm sure all three of us have sat in front of an IDE and wondered what to do with even a single class but a single class that's like seven or 800 lines of code with a small number of huge methods in it.

145
00:15:19,189 --> 00:15:21,750
it's just very hard to change.

146
00:15:21,750 --> 00:15:31,829
So dealing with more smaller pieces and it's bearing those in mind and particularly the emphasis behind this principle is as the principle says leveraging the power of small clearly written by Pierre.

147
00:15:31,829 --> 00:15:33,509
It's a more of an American phrasing of it.

148
00:15:33,509 --> 00:15:43,189
So I mean that that alludes to things like small services not necessarily microservices but you know small services rather than big monoliths with lots of internal interdependencies

149
00:15:43,189 --> 00:15:49,110
or if you're going to build a monolith build it out of really well encapsulated smaller parts that are assembled at runtime.

150
00:15:49,110 --> 00:15:58,069
And if you think about microservices and the whole debates around that, if you combine principle four with actually principle five,

151
00:15:58,069 --> 00:16:04,389
those things together will make you decide what's the best size of those components, right?

152
00:16:04,389 --> 00:16:18,949
because the more if you everything is very small it's very difficult to operate and monitor and manage the dependencies and even just understanding the message flows etc between tens of components versus a few handful is is very different

153
00:16:18,949 --> 00:16:26,629
so I think the principles really add value when you try to apply all of them together definitely and then the last one is the what

154
00:16:26,629 --> 00:16:37,030
you said the modeled organization of the the design of the system this is conway slow is it yes I'd say probably we've probably got less to say about this collectively the three of us than we have about all the others

155
00:16:37,030 --> 00:16:45,509
because it's about organizational design and generally people who are expert software architects don't do organizational design to be honest I think certainly what I've started to point

156
00:16:45,509 --> 00:16:59,829
to is team topologies because I think they've thought quite a lot about a sort of abstracted software engineering approach to how you design organizations which seems to work well in practice they've been adopted in lots of industries but for me actually the core of the principle is bear in mind if you have one structure for your teams

157
00:16:59,839 --> 00:17:08,949
and a different architectural structure in your software the who will start to overlap and merge whether you want them to or not because people need to get things done.

158
00:17:08,949 --> 00:17:12,230
And so if there's lots and lots of discussion needed between teams, they will find ways around that.

159
00:17:12,230 --> 00:17:15,590
And you'll probably find functions bleeding into the wrong modules.

160
00:17:15,590 --> 00:17:19,189
You'll find strange interconnections suddenly appear between components.

161
00:17:19,189 --> 00:17:24,309
You'll find components suddenly getting merged without you being told or split because people can't work on the other one.

162
00:17:24,309 --> 00:17:34,630
So it's if you ignore the relationship between the organizational structure and the software structure, you're fairly doomed to the fact that the two of them are going to start merging in probably unforeseen ways.

163
00:17:34,640 --> 00:17:37,110
Yeah.

164
00:17:37,110 --> 00:17:50,310
to me looks like the Conway law is almost a law of nature of how organizations work and you mentioned team topologies and team topologies is an approach on how to exploit this law somehow make sure that it works in your favor

165
00:17:50,310 --> 00:17:57,510
because it's easy to say you should carry of course is a principle here but modeling the organization after the design of the system

166
00:17:57,510 --> 00:18:07,830
as a principle does not tell you much about how in practice do that and team topologies is an approach for doing that there may be others, but this kind of help you in achieving that.

167
00:18:07,830 --> 00:18:16,470
I guess it's a starting point and like any prescriptive abstract approach, you're probably going to change it in some ways for your particular context, but it's a good starting point.

168
00:18:16,480 --> 00:18:18,870
Yeah. Yeah.

169
00:18:18,870 --> 00:18:20,950
Maybe I'll take the principle in a different way of thinking about it.

170
00:18:20,950 --> 00:18:26,950
It's also about the things you care about and you can change and the things you care about and you can't change.

171
00:18:26,950 --> 00:18:35,430
And if you find yourself that you and this probably works more for if you're thinking of systems of systems or more complex systems

172
00:18:35,430 --> 00:18:50,310
but if you think the perfect system you want to develop but it actually doesn't fit within how your organization is structured for example I mean in banking at the very highest level there's the front office and the back office like if you oversimplify it and if you own the back office system

173
00:18:50,320 --> 00:18:58,390
but for it to be fully successful you need your front office systems to operate differently you really don't have much influence over that.

174
00:18:58,390 --> 00:19:08,470
So they sometimes know what you can influence and if you can't influence it just then build your architecture around that rather trying to fight unnecessary corporate battles.

